## WK5 — First Working Prototype

**Date: 10/12/2026** 

### What I built
- Set up my dev environment: Python virtual environment, Flask, Git, and VS Code
- Connected the project to GitHub and got a first working page running locally
- Built a simple comp-entry form (comp name, key units, rank, notes) that displays saved comps on the page
- Added SQLite so comps are saved to a database and persist after restarting the server

### Decisions
- Started with a plain in-memory list first, then moved to SQLite, so I could confirm the form and display worked before adding a database
- Added a "rank" field to the form on my own, since I want to be able to sort or filter comps by how well they perform

### What went sideways
- **Git and VS Code weren't recognized in the terminal.** Neither was installed or on my PATH yet, so I installed them and restarted my terminal.
- **My first push to GitHub was rejected.** The remote repo already had my README and journal commits, so I had to pull and merge them before pushing.
- **My database file showed up on GitHub.** I'd committed `comps.db` before creating a `.gitignore`, which only affects files Git isn't already tracking. I had to untrack the file with `git rm --cached`, then commit and push the removal. At first I skipped the commit step, which is why it was still on GitHub.

### Reflection
- What surprised me: How simple integrating SQLite was in getting a more permanent storage of the comp. 
- Where I felt stuck, and what helped: I didn't want git tracking the comps.db so figuring out how to do that got me swamped but there were videos and guides on how to fix it that helped. 

